When running Nx commands inside [Claude Code](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/overview)'s sandbox mode, you may see errors related to the Nx daemon, plugin isolation, or forked processes failing to communicate. This happens because Claude Code's sandbox (Seatbelt on macOS, bubblewrap on Linux) blocks Unix socket access by default, and Nx relies on Unix sockets for inter-process communication.

## The fix

Add `allowAllUnixSockets: true` to your Claude Code sandbox network settings. You can set this at the project level (`.claude/settings.local.json`) or user level (`~/.claude/settings.json`):

```json
// .claude/settings.local.json
{
  "sandbox": {
    "network": {
      "allowAllUnixSockets": true
    }
  }
}
```

This grants Nx the ability to create and connect to Unix sockets, which it needs for daemon communication, plugin isolation, and forked task execution.

## Scoped allowlisting with the Nx socket root

Nx creates all of its sockets under a single stable directory on macOS and Linux: `/tmp/.nx/sockets`, unless overridden via `NX_SOCKET_DIR`. Allowlist that one directory, and grant read and write on its parent, instead of granting blanket socket access:

```json
// .claude/settings.json
{
  "sandbox": {
    "filesystem": {
      "allowRead": ["/tmp/.nx"],
      "allowWrite": ["/tmp/.nx"]
    },
    "network": {
      "allowUnixSockets": ["/tmp/.nx/sockets"]
    }
  }
}
```

The path is identical on every machine, so the entries are safe to commit and share with your team.

The write grant covers `/tmp/.nx` rather than only `/tmp/.nx/sockets` because a grant scoped to the socket directory cannot create `/tmp/.nx` itself, so the very first Nx run in a fresh sandbox fails before it reaches the socket. Nx also copies its compiled native binding into a cache under the same root before loading it, which the grant needs to cover for the same reason.

{% aside type="note" title="What keeps these directories private" %}
The allowlist decides which paths the sandbox can reach. It is not what separates users from each other. The per-user directories Nx creates under `/tmp/.nx` are created `0700` and re-checked on each use: owned by you, a real directory rather than a symlink, and carrying no group or other permissions at all — a `0755` directory is tightened back to `0700` rather than accepted. The shared roots themselves (`/tmp/.nx`, `/tmp/.nx/sockets`) are deliberately world-writable and sticky, like `/tmp`, so each user on the machine can create their own private directory beneath them; containment happens one level down, not at the root.

When a directory fails a check that cannot be repaired, Nx refuses it — sockets fall back to a private directory inside your workspace, and the native binding cache is skipped entirely, loading in place from `node_modules` instead.

Code inside the sandbox runs as your user, so this grant does let it write that binary cache, in the same way it can already write your workspace's `node_modules`.
{% /aside %}

Claude Code's `allowUnixSockets` setting currently only allows _connecting_ to sockets, not _creating_ them. A scoped allowlist works when the daemon is already running (started outside the sandbox, for example from your own terminal), but processes inside the sandbox that need to create sockets, such as a fresh daemon, plugin workers, or forked tasks, still require `allowAllUnixSockets: true`.

## Alternative workarounds

If you prefer not to enable blanket Unix socket access, you can disable the Nx features that require sockets. These workarounds come with tradeoffs:

### Disable the Nx daemon

```shell
NX_DAEMON=false nx <command>
```

The [Nx daemon](/docs/concepts/nx-daemon) keeps a persistent process running to speed up repeated operations. Disabling it means Nx starts fresh on every invocation, resulting in slower cold starts (especially in large workspaces).

### Disable plugin isolation

```shell
NX_ISOLATE_PLUGINS=false nx <command>
```

[Plugin isolation](/docs/concepts/inferred-tasks) runs each plugin in a separate forked process to prevent conflicts between multiple TypeScript compilers, module-level caches, and global state. Disabling it runs all plugins in the main process, which can cause issues in workspaces with plugins that have conflicting dependencies.

{% aside type="caution" title="Workarounds are not recommended for regular use" %}
Both environment variables work around the symptom rather than fixing the root cause. The `allowAllUnixSockets` setting is the proper fix and doesn't sacrifice any Nx functionality.
{% /aside %}

## Socket path environment variables

For advanced use cases, Nx provides environment variables to override where socket files are created:

| Variable                         | Description                                                                                                      |
| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `NX_SOCKET_DIR`                  | Overrides the directory for all Nx sockets (daemon, forked process, and plugin). Defaults to `/tmp/.nx/sockets`. |
| `NX_DAEMON_SOCKET_DIR`           | Legacy alias of `NX_SOCKET_DIR`. Used only when `NX_SOCKET_DIR` is not set. Prefer `NX_SOCKET_DIR`.              |
| `NX_NATIVE_FILE_CACHE_DIRECTORY` | Overrides the native file cache path                                                                             |

These are primarily useful for environments with restricted temp directory access (e.g., certain Docker or CI setups). For Claude Code's sandbox, `allowAllUnixSockets: true` is simpler and more reliable.

{% aside type="caution" title="Keep the socket directory private" %}
Do not set `NX_SOCKET_DIR` or `NX_DAEMON_SOCKET_DIR` to a location shared with other users or other workspaces. The daemon drives work inside a long-lived process, so another user who can reach its socket could execute code in your daemon.
{% /aside %}
